  <!-- Intro -->
  <div class="card about-card">
    <h1 style="font-size:1.75rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-0.025em;margin-bottom:0.25rem;">Marina Kuzminchuk</h1>
    <p style="font-size:0.82rem;color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:1.75rem;">UX/UI Designer · Berlin</p>

    <p class="about-intro-text">
      Two years into my engineering career at Cross4Channel, I kept noticing the same pattern: the hardest conversations weren't about how to build something — they were about what it should actually do and how it should feel. I was already contributing to UX decisions: improving layout structure for medical datasets, optimising map interactions, making interface components navigable for healthcare professionals. That attention kept pulling me toward design.
    </p>
    <p class="about-intro-text">
      I made the move deliberate. Now I work from research to handoff — user interviews, flow mapping, wireframing, high-fidelity design, usability testing. My engineering background means I understand implementation constraints, read developer feedback without translation, and design things that are feasible to build.
    </p>
  </div>

